      "claim": "Alpidem, a dipropylamino-substituted heterocycle, had an absolute oral bioavailability of 32 ± 2% in young human subjects, directly above the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C05"],
      "claim": "PNU-96391, a propylpiperidine analogue, had an absolute oral bioavailability of 60% in healthy volunteers after IV and oral administration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C10"],
      "claim": "Tasimelteon, a fused bicyclic amide, had a mean absolute bioavailability of approximately 38% in humans, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Psilocybin, a tryptamine with a tertiary aminoethyl side chain, showed a mean absolute oral bioavailability of 52.7% in humans.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C24"],
      "claim": "Meptazinol, the only low-F analogue, had a mean absolute oral bioavailability of 8.69 ± 1.61% in male volunteers; its low F is driven by extensive phenolic first-pass conjugation, a liability absent in the query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "High F is strongly supported: multiple high-transfer human absolute bioavailability measurements in structurally related N-propylamine and fused bicyclic drugs range from 32% to 80%, all above the 20% threshold. The sole low-F analogue, meptazinol, is mechanistically distinct due to phenolic conjugation, which the query lacks.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule itself is present in the provided evidence.",
    "Some supportive records are not explicitly absolute bioavailability (e.g., C15).",
    "The contradictory meptazinol record has a high transfer score, but its low-F mechanism is not applicable; query-specific first-pass metabolism data would reduce residual uncertainty."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

